---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-dental-health`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Dental Health',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Dental Health', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Dental Health: Caries, Periodontitis, and Implant Osseointegration"
  description="How vitamin D affects tooth enamel, caries risk in children, periodontal disease, and dental implant success — with practical dosing guidance."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-dental-health", label: "Vitamin D & Dental" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Dental Health</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Teeth are calcified tissue and require vitamin D for calcium handling, just like
      bone. Vitamin D also modulates the innate immune response in the periodontium and
      affects cathelicidin-mediated bacterial defence. Deficiency has been linked to
      caries, periodontal disease progression, and implant failure.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Caries in children</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      A 2013 meta-analysis of 24 pediatric studies (2,827 children) reported vitamin D
      supplementation was associated with a 47% reduction in dental caries incidence.
      Effect largest in children with baseline deficiency. Vitamin D contributes to
      formation of the primary and permanent dentition during the pre-eruption window,
      and to salivary immune function post-eruption.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Periodontitis</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      NHANES data (Dietrich 2004) found adults with 25(OH)D in the lowest quintile had
      more alveolar bone loss than those in the highest. Cross-sectional and longitudinal
      studies consistently link lower vitamin D with worse periodontal probing depths and
      attachment loss. Randomised trials of adjunctive vitamin D during periodontal
      treatment are small but favourable.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Dental implants</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Retrospective studies suggest vitamin D deficiency (&lt; 20 ng/mL) is associated with
      higher rates of early implant failure and impaired osseointegration. Some oral
      surgeons check and correct 25(OH)D pre-implant, particularly for grafting and
      sinus-lift cases where bone healing is critical.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Rickets, enamel, and the classic dental sign</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Severe childhood vitamin D deficiency (rickets) causes enamel hypoplasia — visible
      as pitted, discoloured enamel, delayed tooth eruption, and increased caries
      susceptibility. The classic dental sign of nutritional rickets is a defective
      enamel band that indicates when the deficiency occurred developmentally.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical dental guide</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Standard vitamin D adequacy (25(OH)D ≥ 30 ng/mL) supports dental health.</li>
      <li>Fluoride, tooth brushing with fluoridated toothpaste, and dietary sugar restriction remain the primary caries interventions.</li>
      <li>Pregnant and breastfeeding mothers: adequate vitamin D contributes to healthy tooth development in the child.</li>
      <li>Pre-implant vitamin D correction is a reasonable pre-procedure step for deficient patients.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Vitamin D does not replace regular brushing,
      flossing, professional cleanings, or dietary sugar control.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-dental-health" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
